{"hash": "87b8c9fb7e9a12dd", "text": "Across the valley stands a ranger station. A remote is a copy of the map kept there. And git is not GitHub: git is the stones and signposts in your folder; GitHub is one place to keep a copy. In a forum thread this October, an agent's delete took a project's source and its hidden git folder together. An early reply, after \"stop writing to the drive\", said: if it was ever pushed anywhere, clone it back. What mattered was knowing where the copies were. In the real output, asking for remotes prints nothing. After a push there is one, and the next save point shows as \"ahead by one commit\": it exists only on this machine. When there's a copy on GitHub, a cloud session clones that copy, not your laptop, so push first; the lesson on where your agent works has the rest. Here the picture breaks: every git copy is full and equal, and \"the original\" is just the one everyone agrees on.", "alignment": {"characters": ["A", "c", "r", "o", "s", "s", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "v", "a", "l", "l", "e", "y", " ", "s", "t", "a", "n", "d", "s", " ", "a", " ", "r", "a", "n", "g", "e", "r", " ", "s", "t", "a", "t", "i", "o", "n", ".", " ", "A", " ", "r", "e", "m", "o", "t", "e", " ", "i", "s", " ", "a", " ", "c", "o", "p", "y", " ", "o", "f", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "m", "a", "p", " ", "k", "e", "p", "t", " ", "t", "h", "e", "r", "e", ".", " ", "A", "n", "d", " ", "g", "i", "t", " ", "i", "s", " ", "n", "o", "t", " ", "G", "i", "t", "H", "u", "b", ":", " ", "g", "i", "t", " ", "i", "s", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "s", "t", "o", "n", "e", "s", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "s", "i", "g", "n", "p", "o", "s", "t", "s", " ", "i", "n", " ", "y", "o", "u", "r", " ", "f", "o", "l", "d", "e", "r", ";", " ", "G", "i", "t", "H", "u", "b", " ", "i", "s", " ", "o", "n", "e", " ", "p", "l", "a", "c", "e", " ", "t", "o", " ", "k", "e", "e", "p", " ", "a", " ", "c", "o", "p", "y", ".", " ", "I", "n", " ", "a", " ", "f", "o", "r", "u", "m", " ", "t", "h", "r", "e", "a"